## Plan before implementing

For anything beyond a trivial edit, produce a plan first and do not write code until it is
approved. `<OWNER OR ROLE>` decides when building starts -- wait for an explicit go-ahead.

Point at the relevant existing code alongside the plan request. Guessing at an interface you have
not read is the most expensive kind of speed.

Two habits that keep plans honest:

- **Record rejected options with the blocking fact.** "We considered a bare-repo layout; it is
  blocked because `<reason>`" saves the next session the whole cycle. A rejected option with no
  reason gets re-proposed forever.
- **Label an inference as an inference.** If you did not measure it, say so. A wrong failure-mode
  expectation propagates into every session that reads the doc afterwards.

